-- allium: 3
-- orchestrators.allium
--
-- Scope: The orchestrator of an epic. An epic can have an agent of its own
--        that starts the epic's tasks, watches their agents, answers their
--        questions and tells the operator when a person is needed. It writes
--        no code and finishes no task.
-- Includes: The switch on an epic and where it may be on; the ready mark on a
--           backlog task and the epic's start rule; the orchestrator's
--           session on each machine and when it runs; which tasks it may
--           start and how many at once; what it may answer for the operator
--           and what it must pass on; what it does about a task agent in
--           trouble; the epic's log, its notes and the items that wait for
--           the operator; what the board, the notifications and the MCP tools
--           show of all this.
-- Excludes: The dispatch request a start goes through (store-split.allium:
--           RequestDispatch) and its provisioning (dispatch.allium); how a
--           task agent's trouble is detected (agent-health.allium); how a
--           task finishes (pr-workflow.allium: wrap-up, and a review task's
--           own approve or merge); the knowledge base (learnings.allium),
--           which this spec leaves as it is.
-- Dependencies: Epic, Task, Agent, Host, TmuxWindow and core/local_host()
--               from core.allium; DispatchRequested and DispatchRequest from
--               store-split.allium.
--
-- STATUS: SPECIFIED AHEAD OF ITS CODE. This file states behaviour that is
-- decided and not yet built. Every other spec still describes the system as
-- it runs. Each build step moves the facts it implements into the specs that
-- own them (the list is under "What the build changes elsewhere", at the
-- end) in the same change as its code.
--
-- Decisions taken with the user, recorded so they are not re-litigated:
--   - O1, what orchestration is. The orchestrator starts tasks, watches the
--     task agents, answers their questions, and reports to the operator when
--     a person is needed or the epic is finished. It does not split an epic
--     into tasks.
--   - O2, it never does a task's work. It edits no code, has no worktree, and
--     never merges, rebases, approves, closes or deletes. Each task's own
--     agent finishes its task, by wrap-up on any path, and a review task
--     approves or merges its PR as it does without an orchestrator. How
--     automatic an epic is follows from how its tasks are set up.
--   - O3, a switch per epic, off by default, allowed on any epic at any
--     depth, feed epics included (the Bot PRs epic is the first use).
--   - O4, one orchestrator on any path from a root epic to a leaf. It covers
--     its epic's whole subtree. Switching on is refused while an epic above
--     or below has one. Nested orchestrators may be allowed later.
--   - O5, the ready mark. The orchestrator starts only what is wanted. A
--     backlog task is ready or not, and a new task is not. The operator sets
--     the mark on the board; an agent sets it through MCP, at creation or
--     later. The mark states intent and locks nothing: an agent marks a task
--     ready only when the work is already wanted. An empty backlog is not a
--     goal.
--   - O6, the start rule. An epic starts marked tasks only (the default), or
--     everything, where every backlog task counts as ready (a feed of work
--     that should all be handled).
--   - O7, at most three task agents at once per epic, changeable per epic.
--     One limit for every start in an orchestrated epic, the operator's
--     included. Only a running task with a live session counts: a task in
--     review or a detached one takes no slot.
--   - O8, it replaces auto dispatch, which is removed. An orchestrator is the
--     one way tasks start by themselves.
--   - O9, the answer line. See OrchestratorAnswerLine.
--   - O10, one retry, then the operator. See the trouble rules.
--   - O11, one user. Each machine that runs the board runs its own
--     orchestrator for the epic and handles only the tasks on that machine.
--     Nothing picks one machine.
--   - O12, it runs while there is open work and the board runs. It ends when
--     the epic has no open work and comes back when work does.
--   - O13, its memory is in the store: the epic's log, the epic's notes and
--     the items that wait for the operator. It must work from a fresh start.
--   - O17, notes and learnings. An epic note is the orchestrator's working
--     memory: a to-do or a fact about this epic's work now, with a done tick.
--     A learning is what stays true and useful after the epic is done. The
--     orchestrator uses the knowledge base as any agent does: it is given the
--     learnings that fit its epic, and it may search, record and rate.
--   - O14, the board. Tab cycles flat, then flat and active only, then the
--     orchestrator view; the board opens on flat. The orchestrator view
--     replaces the epic-grouped view. In it an orchestrated epic has one
--     card, placed by its orchestrator's state.
--   - O15, one key in the epic view opens the orchestrator's window, or
--     offers to start an orchestrator when the epic has none.
--   - O16, notifications are for an item that waits for the operator and for
--     a finished epic, and nothing else.

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./store-split.allium" as split

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Which backlog tasks an orchestrator may start (O6).
enum StartRule { marked_only | everything }

-- Who set a ready mark.
enum ReadyMarker { operator | agent }

enum LogEntryKind {
    started_task     -- it asked for a task to be started
    answered         -- it answered a task agent's question
    passed_on        -- it passed a question or a problem to the operator
    acted_on_trouble -- it pinged, resumed, retried or redirected a task agent
    session          -- its own session started, resumed or ended
    summary          -- its closing summary of a finished epic
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- O7. An epic's own limit, when set, replaces it.
    orchestrator_max_parallel: Integer = 3
    -- How long the orchestrator waits for a stale agent to answer its ping
    -- before it passes the task to the operator.
    orchestrator_ping_wait: Duration = 10.minutes
    -- How long a task may wait in review before the orchestrator reminds the
    -- operator, once.
    orchestrator_review_reminder_after: Duration = 24.hours
    -- How many log entries the epic view shows, newest first.
    orchestrator_log_shown: Integer = 10
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The switch (O3). An epic is orchestrated while it has one of these.
entity EpicOrchestration {
    epic: core/Epic
    start_rule: StartRule
    max_parallel: Integer?   -- null follows config.orchestrator_max_parallel
    switched_on_at: Timestamp

    limit: if max_parallel != null: max_parallel else: config.orchestrator_max_parallel
}

-- The ready mark (O5). A backlog task is ready while it has one.
entity ReadyMark {
    task: core/Task
    marked_by: ReadyMarker
    agent_name: String?      -- the marking agent's name when marked_by = agent
    marked_at: Timestamp
}

-- An epic's orchestrator on one machine (O11). The row keeps the name and
-- whatever is needed to resume the same conversation; the session is live
-- while it holds a window.
entity Orchestrator {
    orchestration: EpicOrchestration
    host: core/Host
    name: String             -- an agent name, from the pool task agents use
    tmux_window: core/TmuxWindow?
    started_at: Timestamp?   -- when the session it now holds started
    restarted_once: Boolean = false  -- a session that died was started again and has not yet done a turn

    epic: orchestration.epic
    is_live: tmux_window != null
}

-- One line of the epic's log (O13). Append-only.
entity OrchestratorLogEntry {
    epic: core/Epic
    host: core/Host          -- the machine whose orchestrator wrote it
    at: Timestamp
    kind: LogEntryKind
    task: core/Task?         -- the task it is about; an answer always names one
    text: String             -- one line: what was decided or done, and why
    question: String?        -- for answered and passed_on: what was asked
}

-- Something the orchestrator keeps for the epic across its sessions: a
-- decision that binds later tasks, a fact about the epic's work (O13).
entity EpicNote {
    epic: core/Epic
    text: String
    done: Boolean = false    -- a to-do that is finished; a plain fact never is
    written_at: Timestamp
}

-- An item that waits for the operator (O1). Open until resolved.
entity WaitingItem {
    epic: core/Epic
    host: core/Host
    task: core/Task?         -- null when it is about the epic as a whole
    about: String            -- the question or the problem
    tried: String            -- what the orchestrator already did about it
    recommendation: String   -- what it would do
    raised_at: Timestamp
    resolved_at: Timestamp?

    is_open: resolved_at = null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Derived facts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- orchestration_of(epic) is the EpicOrchestration of epic, or null.
-- covering(epic) is the orchestration of epic or of its nearest ancestor that
-- has one, or null. covering(task) is covering(task.epic), null for a task
-- with no epic. By OneOrchestratorOnAPath there is never a choice.
--
-- has_open_work(epic): some task in the epic's subtree is not done.
-- running_here(epic, host): the tasks in the epic's subtree with status =
-- running whose current agent is live on this host. A task in review, and a
-- running task whose agent is detached (no window), are not among them (O7).
-- counts_as_ready(task): the task has a ReadyMark, or covering(task) has
-- start_rule = everything.
-- wanted(orchestrator): its epic has open work.
-- orchestrator_state(orchestrator), what the operator sees of it:
--   working: its session is live and a task of the epic runs here.
--   idle:    its session is live and none does.
--   resting: the epic has no open work, so no session is wanted.
--   down:    a session is wanted and none is live.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == The switch ==

rule SwitchOrchestratorOn {
    when: OrchestratorSwitchedOn(epic, start_rule?, max_parallel?)

    requires: orchestration_of(epic) = null
    -- O4. The refusal names the epic that already has one.
    requires: not epic.subtree_epics.any(e => orchestration_of(e) != null)
    requires: covering(epic) = null
    requires: max_parallel = null or max_parallel >= 1

    ensures: EpicOrchestration.created(
        epic: epic,
        start_rule: if start_rule != null: start_rule else: marked_only,
        max_parallel: max_parallel,
        switched_on_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- One store write. The refusals read "epic <id> already has an
        -- orchestrator" and "epic <id> (<title>) above this one has an
        -- orchestrator" or "... below this one ...", and write nothing.
}

rule ChangeOrchestratorSettings {
    when: OrchestratorSettingsChanged(epic, start_rule?, max_parallel?)

    requires: orchestration_of(epic) != null
    requires: max_parallel = null or max_parallel >= 1

    ensures:
        if start_rule != null: orchestration_of(epic).start_rule = start_rule
    ensures:
        if max_parallel != null: orchestration_of(epic).max_parallel = max_parallel

    @guidance
        -- A lower limit stops no task that already runs; it only holds back
        -- the next start.
}

rule SwitchOrchestratorOff {
    when: OrchestratorSwitchedOff(epic)

    requires: orchestration_of(epic) != null

    let orchestration = orchestration_of(epic)

    -- Every machine's session ends. The task agents run on and ask the
    -- operator again.
    ensures:
        for o in Orchestrators where orchestration = orchestration:
            not exists o
    ensures: not exists orchestration
    ensures:
        for item in WaitingItems where epic = epic and is_open:
            item.resolved_at = now

    @guidance
        -- The log and the notes stay with the epic, so a later switch-on
        -- starts with them. Ready marks stay too. Each board closes the
        -- window of the orchestrator it ran when it sees the row go.
}

rule ReparentingKeepsOneOrchestratorOnAPath {
    when: EpicReparented(epic, new_parent)

    -- The moved epic lands under an orchestrated epic: the orchestrator
    -- above takes its subtree over, and every switch inside it goes off.
    ensures:
        if new_parent != null and covering(new_parent) != null:
            for e in epic.subtree_epics + {epic}:
                if orchestration_of(e) != null:
                    OrchestratorSwitchedOff(e)

    @guidance
        -- In the same write as the move. The reply to the move says which
        -- orchestrators were switched off.
}

rule DeletingAnEpicRemovesItsOrchestration {
    when: EpicDeleted(epic)

    ensures:
        if orchestration_of(epic) != null: OrchestratorSwitchedOff(epic)
    ensures:
        for entry in OrchestratorLogEntries where epic = epic: not exists entry
    ensures:
        for note in EpicNotes where epic = epic: not exists note
    ensures:
        for item in WaitingItems where epic = epic: not exists item
}

-- == The ready mark ==

rule MarkTaskReady {
    when: TaskMarkedReady(task, marked_by, agent_name?)

    requires: task.status = backlog

    ensures:
        if not exists ReadyMark{task: task}:
            ReadyMark.created(
                task: task,
                marked_by: marked_by,
                agent_name: agent_name,
                marked_at: now
            )

    @guidance
        -- Marking a task that is already ready changes nothing and is not an
        -- error; the first marker stays recorded. A task outside backlog is
        -- refused with "only a backlog task can be marked ready". A task may
        -- be marked whether or not an orchestrator covers it: the mark waits.
}

rule ClearReadyMark {
    when: TaskReadyCleared(task)

    ensures: not exists ReadyMark{task: task}
}

rule LeavingBacklogDropsTheReadyMark {
    when: task: core/Task.status transitions_to running

    -- The mark said "start this". Once started it has been used: a task that
    -- comes back to backlog is not ready until it is marked again.
    ensures: not exists ReadyMark{task: task}
}

-- == The session ==

rule BoardRunsItsOrchestrators {
    when: BoardChecksOrchestrators(host)

    -- Each running board, on its tick, for every orchestrated epic.
    ensures:
        for orchestration in EpicOrchestrations:
            if has_open_work(orchestration.epic)
               and not exists Orchestrator{orchestration: orchestration, host: host}:
                Orchestrator.created(
                    orchestration: orchestration,
                    host: host,
                    name: fresh_agent_name(),
                    tmux_window: null,
                    started_at: null
                )
    ensures:
        for o in Orchestrators where host = host:
            if wanted(o) and not o.is_live:
                OrchestratorSessionWanted(o)
            if not wanted(o) and o.is_live:
                OrchestratorSessionUnwanted(o)

    @guidance
        -- HOST-SIDE. This is what "always running" means (O12): while the
        -- board runs and the epic has open work, a missing session is started
        -- again without asking, whatever ended it. A board start is the same
        -- check, so every wanted orchestrator comes back with the board.
        -- A board that does not run, runs none.
}

rule StartOrchestratorSession {
    when: OrchestratorSessionWanted(orchestrator)

    requires: orchestrator.host = core/local_host()
    requires: not orchestrator.is_live
    -- O10 applied to itself: one restart without a completed turn between.
    requires: not orchestrator.restarted_once or not session_died(orchestrator)

    ensures: orchestrator.tmux_window = orchestrator_window(orchestrator)
    ensures: orchestrator.started_at = now
    ensures:
        if session_died(orchestrator): orchestrator.restarted_once = true
    ensures: OrchestratorLogEntry.created(
        epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, at: now,
        kind: session, task: null, question: null,
        text: session_start_text(orchestrator)
    )

    @guidance
        -- The session resumes the conversation this machine last held for the
        -- epic when it can, and starts fresh otherwise. Either way it gets
        -- the prompt (OrchestratorPrompt), so a fresh start loses nothing
        -- that matters (O13).
        -- It runs in a directory of its own under the data directory, not in
        -- a worktree and not in a repository (O2). Its session carries the
        -- orchestrator's name, as a task agent's does, so agents and the
        -- operator reach it by that name.
        -- session_died: the last session ended without the board ending it.
}

rule OrchestratorThatKeepsDyingWaitsForTheOperator {
    when: OrchestratorSessionWanted(orchestrator)

    requires: orchestrator.host = core/local_host()
    requires: not orchestrator.is_live
    requires: orchestrator.restarted_once and session_died(orchestrator)
    requires: not exists WaitingItem{epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, task: null, resolved_at: null}

    ensures: WaitingItemRaised(
        epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, task: null,
        about: "the orchestrator's session died twice in a row",
        tried: "restarted it once",
        recommendation: "open its window, or start it again from the epic view"
    )

    @guidance
        -- It then shows as down. The operator starts it again with the
        -- orchestrator key (OrchestratorKey), which clears restarted_once.
}

rule OrchestratorTurnClearsTheRestartFlag {
    when: OrchestratorCompletedATurn(orchestrator)

    ensures: orchestrator.restarted_once = false
}

rule EndOrchestratorSession {
    when: OrchestratorSessionUnwanted(orchestrator)

    requires: orchestrator.host = core/local_host()
    requires: orchestrator.is_live

    ensures: orchestrator.tmux_window = null
    ensures:
        if orchestrator.epic.status = done:
            OrchestratorLogEntry.created(
                epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, at: now,
                kind: summary, task: null, question: null,
                text: closing_summary(orchestrator)
            )
            EpicFinishedNotified(orchestrator.epic)

    @guidance
        -- No open work is the one condition (O12): a finished epic, and an
        -- epic that is not done but has nothing open, such as a feed with
        -- nothing to handle. The orchestrator is asked for its closing
        -- summary before its window closes. A task that reopens or arrives
        -- makes the session wanted again on the next check.
}

-- == Starting tasks ==

rule OrchestratorStartsATask {
    when: OrchestratorStartsTask(orchestrator, task)

    let orchestration = orchestrator.orchestration

    requires: covering(task) = orchestration
    requires: task.status = backlog
    requires: counts_as_ready(task)
    requires: running_here(orchestrator.epic, orchestrator.host).count < orchestration.limit

    ensures: split/DispatchRequested(
        task: task,
        requester: orchestrator.host,
        origin: mcp
    )
    ensures: OrchestratorLogEntry.created(
        epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, at: now,
        kind: started_task, task: task, question: null,
        text: start_reason(orchestrator, task)
    )

    @guidance
        -- The start is an ordinary dispatch request, so every refusal of
        -- RequestDispatch applies as it stands: a task another machine holds,
        -- a task with an open request, a phoenix task, a task still waiting
        -- for its scheduled time. Two machines' orchestrators that pick the
        -- same task race on that request and the store accepts one.
        --
        -- The requires above are the store's, not only the prompt's: a start
        -- by an orchestrator is refused for a task that is not ready or not
        -- in its subtree, with a message that says which. The limit is every
        -- start's (StartsInAnOrchestratedEpicShareOneLimit).
        --
        -- WHICH ready task first is the orchestrator's judgement: it reads
        -- the tasks and the plan and holds back a task that depends on
        -- unfinished work. Between tasks it cannot tell apart it takes board
        -- order (sort_order, then id), as the chain did.
        --
        -- It starts work when it wakes (OrchestratorWakeEvents) and a slot is
        -- free. A task that is not ready is never started; the orchestrator
        -- may tell the operator that one looks unblocked.
}

rule StartsInAnOrchestratedEpicShareOneLimit {
    when: StartAsked(task, host)

    -- O7. Every way a task of an orchestrated epic starts on a machine, the
    -- operator's own key included, is held to the one limit. A task no
    -- orchestrator covers has none.
    requires: covering(task) = null
        or running_here(covering(task).epic, host).count < covering(task).limit

    ensures: StartAllowed(task, host)

    @guidance
        -- Once built this is a requires of RequestDispatch itself, with no
        -- exception for who asks. The refusal reads "epic <id> already runs
        -- <n> of <limit> tasks". A resume of a task that already runs is not
        -- a start and is not held to it. Because a task in review and a
        -- detached task take no slot, an agent that waits for a PR review
        -- does not hold up the next task.
}

-- == Answering ==

rule OrchestratorAnswersAnAgent {
    when: OrchestratorAnswered(orchestrator, task, question, answer, reason)

    requires: covering(task) = orchestrator.orchestration

    ensures: OrchestratorLogEntry.created(
        epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, at: now,
        kind: answered, task: task, question: question,
        text: answer_line(answer, reason)
    )

    @guidance
        -- The answer itself travels as a message to the task agent. The log
        -- entry is how "every answer is recorded on the task" holds: the
        -- task's detail lists the entries that name it.
}

rule OrchestratorPassesOnToTheOperator {
    when: WaitingItemRaised(epic, host, task?, about, tried, recommendation)

    ensures: WaitingItem.created(
        epic: epic, host: host, task: task,
        about: about, tried: tried, recommendation: recommendation,
        raised_at: now, resolved_at: null
    )
    ensures: OrchestratorLogEntry.created(
        epic: epic, host: host, at: now,
        kind: passed_on, task: task, question: about,
        text: recommendation
    )
    ensures:
        if notifications_enabled:
            DesktopNotification(epic: epic, task: task, about: waits_for_operator)

    @guidance
        -- One notification per item, when it is raised (O16).
}

rule WaitingItemResolved {
    when: WaitingItemResolved(item)

    requires: item.is_open

    ensures: item.resolved_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Three ways in. The operator resolves it on the board or by telling
        -- the orchestrator. The orchestrator resolves it when the operator
        -- has answered. And the store resolves it when its task is done or
        -- deleted. Nothing resolves it on a timer: an item raised at night is
        -- still there in the morning.
}

rule FinishedTaskResolvesItsItems {
    when: task: core/Task.status transitions_to done

    ensures:
        for item in WaitingItems where task = task and is_open:
            item.resolved_at = now
}

-- == Notes ==

rule WriteEpicNote {
    when: EpicNoteWritten(epic, text)

    requires: text != ""

    ensures: EpicNote.created(epic: epic, text: text, written_at: now)

    @guidance
        -- What the orchestrator would otherwise carry in its head and repeat
        -- in its replies: a decision that binds later tasks, a shared fact
        -- that moves while the epic runs (the next free version number, a
        -- name another task introduced), a step nobody owns yet. It puts the
        -- notes that fit into its brief to a task agent it starts or answers.
        -- A task agent sends what it learns to the orchestrator, which
        -- decides whether it is a note.
        --
        -- O17: what will still be true and useful after the epic is done is
        -- a learning, recorded in the knowledge base as any agent records
        -- one; the rest is a note. The closing summary of a finished epic
        -- names the notes that look worth keeping as learnings.
}

rule ChangeEpicNote {
    when: EpicNoteChanged(note, text?, done?)

    ensures:
        if text != null: note.text = text
    ensures:
        if done != null: note.done = done

    @guidance
        -- A to-do is ticked done and stays readable until the orchestrator
        -- removes it. An open to-do that only a person can do is also raised
        -- as a WaitingItem, so the operator sees it.
}

rule RemoveEpicNote {
    when: EpicNoteRemoved(note)

    ensures: not exists note

    @guidance
        -- A note that stopped being true is removed, not kept beside its
        -- correction. The orchestrator sees the epic change, so it is the one
        -- to notice.
}

-- == Trouble ==

-- Each rule fires when the orchestrator, woken by the event, acts on it. A
-- board with no live orchestrator for the task does what it does without one.

rule AgentNeedsInput {
    when: task: core/Task.sub_status transitions_to needs_input

    requires: live_orchestrator_for(task) != null

    ensures: OrchestratorAsked(live_orchestrator_for(task), task)

    @guidance
        -- The orchestrator reads what the agent is asking and either answers
        -- (OrchestratorAnswersAnAgent) or passes it on, by
        -- OrchestratorAnswerLine. The task keeps its needs-input state on the
        -- board until the agent works again.
}

rule AgentIsStale {
    when: task: core/Task.sub_status transitions_to stale

    requires: live_orchestrator_for(task) != null

    ensures: AgentPinged(live_orchestrator_for(task), task)

    @guidance
        -- A message asking for status. An agent that works again within
        -- config.orchestrator_ping_wait ends it there.
}

rule StaleAgentDidNotAnswer {
    when: AgentPingExpired(orchestrator, task)

    requires: task.sub_status = stale

    ensures: WaitingItemRaised(
        epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, task: task,
        about: "the agent is stale and did not answer",
        tried: "asked it for status",
        recommendation: stale_recommendation(orchestrator, task)
    )
}

rule AgentCrashed {
    when: task: core/Task.sub_status transitions_to crashed

    requires: live_orchestrator_for(task) != null

    let orchestrator = live_orchestrator_for(task)

    ensures:
        if not already_retried(orchestrator, task):
            TaskResumeRequested(task)
        else:
            WaitingItemRaised(
                epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, task: task,
                about: "the agent crashed again after a resume",
                tried: "resumed it once",
                recommendation: crash_recommendation(orchestrator, task)
            )

    @guidance
        -- already_retried: the log holds an acted_on_trouble entry for this
        -- task and this kind of trouble since the task last entered running.
        -- The resume writes that entry.
}

rule StartFailed {
    when: request: split/DispatchRequest.state transitions_to failed

    requires: live_orchestrator_for(request.task) != null
    requires: started_by_orchestrator(request)

    let orchestrator = live_orchestrator_for(request.task)

    ensures:
        if not already_retried(orchestrator, request.task):
            OrchestratorStartsTask(orchestrator, request.task)
        else:
            WaitingItemRaised(
                epic: orchestrator.epic, host: orchestrator.host, task: request.task,
                about: "the task could not be started",
                tried: "started it twice",
                recommendation: request.reason
            )

    @guidance
        -- The failed request has released the task to backlog already
        -- (store-split.allium: ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest). Its ready mark
        -- went when it left backlog, so the retry marks it ready again first.
}

rule WrapUpConflict {
    when: task: core/Task.sub_status transitions_to conflict

    requires: live_orchestrator_for(task) != null

    ensures: AgentToldToResolveConflict(live_orchestrator_for(task), task)

    @guidance
        -- A message to the task agent: resolve the conflict and wrap up
        -- again. The orchestrator resolves nothing itself (O2). A second
        -- conflict on the same task is passed to the operator.
}

rule LongWaitInReview {
    when: task: core/Task.status_changed_at + config.orchestrator_review_reminder_after <= now

    requires: task.status = review
    requires: live_orchestrator_for(task) != null
    requires: not exists WaitingItem{task: task, resolved_at: null}

    ensures: WaitingItemRaised(
        epic: live_orchestrator_for(task).epic,
        host: live_orchestrator_for(task).host,
        task: task,
        about: "the task has waited in review",
        tried: "nothing: a review is the operator's",
        recommendation: review_recommendation(task)
    )

    @guidance
        -- Once per stay in review. The task is not moved.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- O4.
invariant OneOrchestratorOnAPath {
    for a in EpicOrchestrations: for b in EpicOrchestrations:
        a != b implies (a.epic not in b.epic.subtree_epics)
}

invariant OneOrchestrationPerEpic {
    for a in EpicOrchestrations: for b in EpicOrchestrations:
        a.epic = b.epic implies a = b
}

-- O11. One per epic and machine.
invariant OneOrchestratorPerEpicAndHost {
    for a in Orchestrators: for b in Orchestrators:
        (a.orchestration = b.orchestration and a.host = b.host) implies a = b
}

-- An orchestrator's name is no task agent's name, and no other
-- orchestrator's: a message to a name reaches one session.
invariant OrchestratorNamesAreUnique {
    for o in Orchestrators:
        not core/Agents.any(a => a.name = o.name)
        and not Orchestrators.any(p => p != o and p.name = o.name)
}

-- O5. Only a backlog task is ready.
invariant OnlyBacklogTasksAreReady {
    for m in ReadyMarks:
        m.task.status = backlog
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What may be decided for the operator, and what may not (O9). This is the
-- text of the orchestrator's prompt as much as it is a rule.
surface OrchestratorAnswerLine {
    facing agent: core/DispatchedAgent

    @guarantee MayAnswer
        -- Questions about the work: scope, approach, how to read the task or
        -- the plan. A plan confirmation, when the plan fits the task and the
        -- epic's goal. The wrap-up path, when the task or the epic already
        -- states a preferred one. "May I approve or merge this PR?" from a
        -- review task, when its review found nothing blocking and the checks
        -- pass. Coordination between agents: who owns a file, who waits for
        -- whom.

    @guarantee MustPassOn
        -- Anything destructive or hard to undo outside the task's own
        -- worktree. A change to what the epic is for, or work its description
        -- does not cover. New credentials, new external services, spending.
        -- Anything it is not confident about. Any question where the agent
        -- and the orchestrator still disagree after one round.

    @guarantee LeadsWithADecision
        -- An answer is a decision and a one-line reason, not a menu.

    @guarantee NeverDoesTheWork
        -- O2. It edits no file of any task, runs no wrap-up, merges,
        -- approves, closes or deletes nothing. It talks to agents and uses
        -- the dispatch tools.

    @guidance
        -- A question passed on raises a WaitingItem with the question, what
        -- the orchestrator tried and what it recommends. The operator may
        -- answer the orchestrator or the task agent; either way the answer
        -- reaches the task agent, and the item is resolved.
}

-- What wakes a live orchestrator. Each arrives as a message in its session.
surface OrchestratorWakeEvents {
    facing agent: core/DispatchedAgent

    @guarantee WokenByItsSubtreeOnly
        -- A task appears in, or moves into, the subtree. A task is marked
        -- ready or its mark is cleared. A task of this machine changes status
        -- or enters needs input, stale, crashed or conflict. A start the
        -- orchestrator asked for fails. A ping or a review reminder comes
        -- due. A task agent or the operator writes to it. A WaitingItem of
        -- the epic is resolved.

    @guarantee QuietOtherwise
        -- Between events it does nothing and costs nothing. It does not poll
        -- the board.

    @guarantee AnUndeliveredReplyIsNotLost
        -- A message to a task agent whose session is not there to take it is
        -- reported back to the orchestrator as undelivered
        -- (agent-health.allium: UndeliveredPeerMessage). The answer is in the
        -- log either way, and the agent's next prompt points it there.

    @guarantee OnlyThisMachinesAgents
        -- O11. It sees every task of the subtree, and acts on the agents of
        -- its own machine. A task another machine runs is that machine's
        -- orchestrator's.
}

-- What the orchestrator is told when its session starts, fresh or resumed.
surface OrchestratorPrompt {
    facing agent: core/DispatchedAgent

    context orchestrator: Orchestrator

    exposes:
        orchestrator.name
        orchestrator.epic.title
        orchestrator.epic.description
        orchestrator.epic.plan_path
        orchestrator.orchestration.start_rule
        orchestrator.orchestration.limit
        orchestrator.epic.subtree_tasks
        EpicNotes where epic = orchestrator.epic
        WaitingItems where epic = orchestrator.epic and is_open
        OrchestratorLogEntries where epic = orchestrator.epic

    @guarantee SaysWhatItIs
        -- That it orchestrates this epic and does none of its work (O1, O2),
        -- the answer line (OrchestratorAnswerLine), the start rule and the
        -- limit, and the trouble rules with their one retry (O10).

    @guarantee GivenItsLearnings
        -- O17. The prompt carries the learnings that fit the epic, chosen as
        -- for a task of that epic, and the one sentence that tells a note
        -- from a learning. It rates what it acts on, as a task agent does.

    @guarantee EnoughForAFreshStart
        -- Each task of the subtree with its id, title, status, sub-status,
        -- ready mark and agent name; every note; every open item; and the
        -- newest log entries. The rest of the log is read through MCP.

    @guarantee FirstTurnChecksTheBoard
        -- On a start it looks for what happened while no session ran: agents
        -- that need input, ready tasks with a free slot, open items the
        -- operator has answered.
}

-- What a task agent is told when an orchestrator covers its task.
surface TaskAgentUnderAnOrchestrator {
    facing agent: core/DispatchedAgent

    @guarantee AsksTheOrchestratorFirst
        -- The task's prompt names the orchestrator and says: send your
        -- questions, plan confirmations included, to it and not to the
        -- operator; tell it what you learn that later tasks of the epic
        -- need. A prompt for a task no orchestrator covers says none of
        -- this.

    @guarantee QuestionsHaveOneShape
        -- The prompt gives the shape of a question to the orchestrator: what
        -- the agent plans, the question, the options, the one it recommends,
        -- and where in the code or the spec the evidence is. It also asks for
        -- a short outcome report when the task finishes.

    @guarantee FallsBackToTheOperator
        -- When no orchestrator is live for the task on this machine, the
        -- agent asks the operator, as an agent without one does.

    @guarantee MarksReadyOnlyWhatIsWanted
        -- O5. Task-creating guidance says: create a task as ready only when
        -- the operator asked for the work or it is a step of the epic's
        -- plan. A new idea or a side finding is created not ready.
}

-- The MCP side. Every board action here has a tool, so an agent can do what
-- the operator can.
surface OrchestratorMcpTools {
    facing _: core/McpClient

    provides:
        -- epic_update: orchestrator on or off, start rule, limit.
        OrchestratorSwitchedOn(epic, start_rule?, max_parallel?)
        OrchestratorSwitchedOff(epic)
        OrchestratorSettingsChanged(epic, start_rule?, max_parallel?)
        -- task_create and task_update: the ready argument. Optional on
        -- create, where leaving it out means not ready.
        TaskMarkedReady(task, marked_by, agent_name?)
        TaskReadyCleared(task)
        -- The orchestrator's own writes.
        OrchestratorStartsTask(orchestrator, task)
        OrchestratorAnswered(orchestrator, task, question, answer, reason)
        WaitingItemRaised(epic, host, task?, about, tried, recommendation)
        WaitingItemResolved(item)
        EpicNoteWritten(epic, text)
        EpicNoteChanged(note, text?, done?)
        EpicNoteRemoved(note)
        -- Any start of a task, by any tool or key.
        StartAsked(task, host)

    @guarantee EpicReadsShowTheOrchestrator
        -- epic_search for one epic says whether it is orchestrated, its
        -- start rule and limit, each machine's orchestrator by name and
        -- state, the open items, the notes and the newest log entries. A
        -- further read returns the whole log, oldest entries by page.

    @guarantee TaskReadsShowTheMark
        -- A task read says ready or not, who marked it, the orchestrator
        -- that covers it by name, and the log entries that name the task.

    @guarantee OnlyTheOrchestratorWritesItsLog
        -- A start, an answer and an item raised write their own log entry.
        -- No tool appends a free line to the log or edits one.
}

-- The board's third lens (O14).
surface OrchestratorView {
    facing user: core/Operator

    context orchestrator: Orchestrator

    let epic = orchestrator.epic
    let open_items = WaitingItems where epic = epic and is_open
    let state = orchestrator_state(orchestrator)
    let column = orchestrator_column(orchestrator)

    exposes:
        epic.title
        state
        column
        open_items.count
        running_here(epic, orchestrator.host).count
        orchestrator.orchestration.limit

    @guarantee LensOrder
        -- Tab steps flat, flat and active only, orchestrators, and wraps;
        -- BackTab steps back. Every board start opens on flat. The
        -- epic-grouped lens is gone, and with it an epic card in several
        -- columns.

    @guarantee OneCardPerOrchestratedEpic
        -- The view holds one card for each orchestrated epic the board
        -- filter admits, for this machine's orchestrator, and nothing else:
        -- no task cards and no other epics. An orchestrated epic whose
        -- orchestrator has no row here yet shows as resting.

    @guarantee EpicCardInOrchestratorView
        -- The card's column, first match:
        --   review   an item of the epic waits for the operator
        --   running  a task of the epic runs on this machine
        --   done     the epic has tasks and none is open
        --   backlog  otherwise: on, and nothing is started
        -- This is how the card is drawn. It is not the epic's stored status,
        -- which is derived from its children as before.

    @guarantee CardAnswersDoINeedToLook
        -- Title; state (working, idle, resting, down); the count of items
        -- that wait for the operator, the loudest thing on the card and
        -- absent at zero; agents running out of the limit, as "2/3"; task
        -- counts for backlog, ready, running, review and done; the newest
        -- log line, cut to fit. Down is drawn as an alarm, like crashed.

    @guarantee KeysOnTheCard
        -- Enter opens the epic view, flattened, with the epic's tasks. The
        -- orchestrator key opens the orchestrator's window.
}

-- The epic view's part (O14, O15).
surface OrchestratorInEpicView {
    facing _: core/Operator

    context epic: core/Epic

    provides:
        -- The orchestrator key on an epic with no orchestrator above, below
        -- or on it, then y.
        OrchestratorSwitchedOn(epic, start_rule?, max_parallel?)
        -- The ready key on a backlog task card, in any view.
        TaskMarkedReady(task, marked_by, agent_name?)
        TaskReadyCleared(task)
        -- The resolve key on an open item.
        WaitingItemResolved(item)

    @guarantee HeaderShowsTheOrchestrator
        -- In an orchestrated epic's view, and in the view of any epic below
        -- it, the header shows the orchestrator's name and state, the open
        -- items with what each is about, and the newest
        -- config.orchestrator_log_shown log entries, newest first.

    @guarantee OrchestratorKey
        -- One key, in the epic view and on any task card of a covered epic.
        -- With an orchestrator: it opens that orchestrator's window, starting
        -- its session first when it is down. Without one: it asks "Start an
        -- orchestrator for this epic? [y/n]" and y switches it on. Where an
        -- epic above or below already has one it opens that one instead of
        -- asking.

    @guarantee SwitchingOffIsNotAKey
        -- The epic's edit form has an orchestrator section: on or off, the
        -- start rule and the limit. There is no one-key switch-off.

    @guarantee ReadyShowsOnTheCard
        -- A ready backlog card carries a ready marker; when an agent set
        -- it, the marker names that agent.

    @guarantee OrchestratorLeadsItsGroupInTheAgentList
        -- In the agent list an orchestrator is the first row of its epic's
        -- group, marked as the orchestrator.
}

-- O16.
surface OrchestratorNotifications {
    facing user: core/Operator

    @guarantee TwoOccasions
        -- A desktop notification when an item starts to wait for the
        -- operator, naming the epic, the task and what it is about; and one
        -- when an orchestrated epic finishes. Both follow
        -- notifications_enabled. Nothing the orchestrator handles by itself
        -- notifies.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- What the build changes elsewhere
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Each of these is true once its build step lands, and moves to its owner
-- then:
--   - core.allium: the entities above; an orchestrator is an agent that
--     belongs to an epic and has no task and no worktree, which the Agent
--     entity and agents.allium's invariants do not allow for today.
--   - epics.allium: auto dispatch goes (O8): the flag on the epic, its
--     toggle key and header indicator, the chain that started the next
--     subtask when a session closed, its failure report and the stalled-chain
--     mark on a card. Also the re-parent and delete effects, and the epic
--     edit form's orchestrator section.
--   - store-split.allium: the chain's request origin goes, and
--     RequestDispatch gains the limit. schedules.allium and
--     mcp-task-tools.allium lose their mentions of the chain.
--   - tasks.allium and mcp-task-tools.allium: the ready argument, the task
--     and epic reads, and the caller identity of an orchestrator.
--   - dispatch-prompt.allium: the line a covered task's prompt gains, and
--     the orchestrator's own prompt.
--   - agent-health.allium: the hooks of an orchestrator's session, and its
--     wake events.
--   - board-layout.allium, board-visuals.allium, agent-tree.allium and
--     keybindings.allium: the lens order, the orchestrator view, the header,
--     the ready marker and the two keys.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "With the epic-grouped lens gone, an epic is entered from one of its task cards or from its card in Backlog. Is every epic still reachable: one with no tasks, and a sub-epic whose tasks are all outside backlog and hidden by a filter?"

open question "The limit counts running tasks per machine, since each machine has its own orchestrator. Two machines can then run twice the limit for one epic. Is that wanted, or should the limit hold across machines?"

open question "The knowledge base has weak feedback, almost no epic-scoped entries and nothing that expires an entry. With an orchestrator that owns its epic's notes and reads its learnings, should it also curate the epic's learnings, and how is a note promoted to a learning when an epic ends?"

open question "Should a managed epic (Bot PRs) come with its orchestrator switched on when it is provisioned, or does the operator switch it on once by hand?"
